You can chane the path by exporting config ANSIBLE_CONFIG=/home/rakpooja/Ansible/ansible.cfg

in ansible if you pass var in commands will get more prcedence
ansible-playbook playbook.yaml -e "app_port=8080"

in ansible inside the group_vars you can create yaml file matching the host and set the ansible_user and ansible_ssh_private_key_file path
ansible_user:
ansible_ssh_private_key_file:

if you want to set for all hosts you can create 
all.yaml

You can also create the host_vars folder and for specific host you can set the user and file path
host_vars/13.204.46.194.yaml
ansible_user:
ansible_ssh_private_key_file:
This overrides the group_vars


You can also use alias in the hosts
server1 ansible_host=13.204.46.194 
In this case in host_vars file you must use the server1.yaml file name

If ansible controller in same vpc use the pvt ip or you need to use the pub ip

In ansible config we can pass the defaults
like inventory=inventories/hosts then we no need to specify =i and path in ansible-playbook commands

when we write 
  update_cache: yes  it means sudo apt update

  to stop nginx first stop it

  -name: nginx stop
   service:
        name: nginx
        state: stopped
    ignore_errors: yes

    -name :nginx uninstall
     service:
        name: nginx
        state: absent
        purge: yes
        update_cache: yes

to test the connection we use 
ansible hostname -m ping
ansible web -m ping

